From c5e9ec398f9920ed89b13369584ce10848748e82 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jiaxing Hu <gahing@gahingwoo.com>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 14:49:55 +1200
Subject: [PATCH] rocket RK3576: the depthwise weight buffer is channel groups
 of 64

Established offline against vendor .rknn files compiled at 32, 128, 256 and
1024 channels, with weights generated on the host so the test is exact rather
than a correlation:

    group of 64      288/288, 1152/1152, 2304/2304, 9208/9216 weight lanes
    one group of C   288/288 at 32 channels, about 4 in 1000 above it

The eight lanes that miss at 1024 are all one channel, whose kernel the
quantiser treated differently; 1023 of 1024 channels are exact.

So the buffer is channel groups of 64, each group tap major inside itself and
the groups laid end to end. A single group of C, which this file wrote, puts a
channel's nine taps 2C bytes apart, 2048 at 1024 channels; a group of 64 puts
them 128 apart whatever C is. The two are identical up to 64 channels, which is
why every depthwise in this project has been byte exact and the 1024 channel
one has not.

It matches what the board measured, too. With one live tap in every channel the
channels that produced anything were a contiguous run that moved with the tap,
and inverting the nine runs showed each output channel could reach only one or
two of its nine spatial blocks.

The total size is unchanged, 16 groups of 9 x 128 being the same 18432 bytes,
so the CNA weight byte count at 0x101c is untouched.

ROCKET_DW_GROUP overrides the group size; setting it to the channel count
restores the old single group as a control. ROCKET_DW_TAPREV is dropped: it was
built to test a reading of round 63 that rounds 64 and 65 both refuted.

Signed-off-by: Jiaxing Hu <gahing@gahingwoo.com>
---
 src/gallium/drivers/rocket/rkt_coefs.c | 70 +++++++++++++++++---------
 1 file changed, 46 insertions(+), 24 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/gallium/drivers/rocket/rkt_coefs.c b/src/gallium/drivers/rocket/rkt_coefs.c
index 5efe963..1a8861f 100644
--- a/src/gallium/drivers/rocket/rkt_coefs.c
+++ b/src/gallium/drivers/rocket/rkt_coefs.c
@@ -194,38 +194,60 @@ rkt_fill_weights(struct rkt_ml_subgraph *subgraph,
       uint8_t pad = zero_point - 0x80;
       uint8_t zplane = getenv("ROCKET_DW_ZP_POS") ? pad : (uint8_t)(-(int8_t)pad);
       unsigned npos = weights_width * weights_height;
-      unsigned block = DIV_ROUND_UP(channels, 2) * 4;
       /*
-       * ROCKET_DW_TAPREV: write the spatial blocks in reverse cyclic order.
+       * The buffer is CHANNEL GROUPS of 64, each group tap major inside
+       * itself, and the groups laid end to end.
        *
-       * The 1024 channel impulse decodes to a perfect single tap of the right
-       * input plane, correlation +1.000 on every channel and 897 of 1024
-       * reading their OWN plane, but the WRONG tap: measured against the tap
-       * the impulse was placed at, the hardware uses (-p) mod 9. At 32
-       * channels the same buffer and the same code give a byte-exact surface,
-       * so the order cannot be globally wrong. This writes the permutation the
-       * board reported, which turns the observation into a test rather than a
-       * story: if it makes the 1024 channel case correct and breaks the 32
-       * channel one, the traversal depends on something and the next question
-       * is what.
+       * Established offline against vendor .rknn files compiled at 32, 128,
+       * 256 and 1024 channels, whose weights are generated on the host so the
+       * test is exact rather than a correlation: a group of 64 reproduces
+       * 288/288, 1152/1152, 2304/2304 and 9208/9216 weight lanes, and the
+       * eight that miss at 1024 are all one channel whose kernel the quantiser
+       * treated differently. One group of C, which is what this file used to
+       * write, reproduces 288/288 at 32 channels and about 4 in 1000 above it.
+       *
+       * The two agree exactly up to 64 channels, which is why every depthwise
+       * in this project has been byte exact and the 1024 channel one has not.
+       *
+       * It also matches what the board measured. With one live tap in every
+       * channel, the channels that produced anything were a contiguous run
+       * that moved with the tap, and inverting the nine runs showed each
+       * output channel could reach only ONE OR TWO of its nine spatial blocks:
+       * a group of C puts them 2C bytes apart, 2048 at 1024 channels, while a
+       * group of 64 puts them 128 apart whatever C is.
+       *
+       * ROCKET_DW_GROUP overrides the group size. Setting it to the channel
+       * count restores the old single group and is the control.
        */
-      bool taprev = getenv("ROCKET_DW_TAPREV") != NULL;
+      unsigned group = MIN2(channels, 64u);
+      const char *gs = getenv("ROCKET_DW_GROUP");
+      if (gs) {
+         unsigned v = (unsigned)strtoul(gs, NULL, 0);
+         if (v)
+            group = MIN2(channels, v);
+      }
+
+      unsigned block = DIV_ROUND_UP(group, 2) * 4;
+      unsigned gsize = npos * block;
 
       for (int y = 0; y < weights_width; y++) {        /* ky (dims[1]) */
          for (int x = 0; x < weights_height; x++) {     /* kx (dims[2]) */
             unsigned pos = y * weights_height + x;
-            uint8_t *blk =
-               weights_out + (taprev ? (npos - pos) % npos : pos) * block;
-            unsigned n = 0;
-
-            for (unsigned g = 0; g < DIV_ROUND_UP(channels, 2); g++) {
-               for (unsigned s = 0; s < 2; s++) {
-                  unsigned c = g * 2 + s;
-                  blk[n++] =
-                     c < channels ? weights_in[0][y][x][c] - 0x80 : pad;
+
+            for (unsigned c0 = 0; c0 < channels; c0 += group) {
+               uint8_t *blk =
+                  weights_out + (c0 / group) * gsize + pos * block;
+               unsigned n = 0;
+
+               for (unsigned g = 0; g < DIV_ROUND_UP(group, 2); g++) {
+                  for (unsigned s = 0; s < 2; s++) {
+                     unsigned c = c0 + g * 2 + s;
+                     blk[n++] =
+                        c < channels ? weights_in[0][y][x][c] - 0x80 : pad;
+                  }
+                  blk[n++] = zplane;
+                  blk[n++] = zplane;
                }
-               blk[n++] = zplane;
-               blk[n++] = zplane;
             }
          }
       }
-- 
2.43.0

